d29b2-a2a2-52d8-94c1-570c7b1f917d" :question/math-academy-id "q-15716" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/assets/a7a711eed2b4805f0e9dfc64c0992acfef26430e37f8df446b08055143a6cadc.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,5)$ and lies on $y=f.$ The point $B$ has coordinates $(5,2)$ and lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(2)=4,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(5)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B\\,(5,2)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(5)=2.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(2)=4.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-15716/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-15716"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-312578"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/assets/e00b5083b25408240fb0eef26c898e237437bf9fe0f7fce11e397b9d7683a1c2.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(0,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-15719/selection/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d" :answer/id #uuid "176a76e3-386b-51ea-be11-acdb252362af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15719/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "82c76660-31ae-5aff-b80d-41e76740bcdf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15719/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "ff8b1edd-a11b-5cd3-a263-27bbffb1d39a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15719/selection/09a797f0254edd22776128e046edd3557657fb445dcc367842699960b6d091f0" :answer/id #uuid "2224d98b-c515-5ca1-8172-2c517793da4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15719/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "2246cda6-4be2-516f-bcb4-ca244d23ba68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15719/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6e7ec39b-c724-5e50-93d1-f69e7fa49ca8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-15719/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-15719/selection/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d" "q-15719/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-15719/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" "q-15719/selection/09a797f0254edd22776128e046edd3557657fb445dcc367842699960b6d091f0" "q-15719/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-15719" :question/id #uuid "01aa62d5-9416-5775-b66b-204e3e2095b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-15719" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/assets/517276d5122f1a81b894da7be7796c5941a16442987f99f476220979bc5688c7.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(3,2.6)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ The point $B$ has coordinates $(2.6,3)$ and lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{3},$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2.6)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B\\,(2.6,3)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2.6)=3.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2.6) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2.6))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-15719/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-15719"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342562/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "9279453a-70a9-5b54-8d67-c3311cf7e8b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186295823 :answer-field/choices ["q-342562/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-342562"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/assets/08ce8625dcad742776846497c4e49b5686a893be85ff1bd5426df8f8b9ce882b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,1)$ and lies on both $y=f(x)$ and $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(1)=-2,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-15667"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/assets/24d83be9fed627018111aff41b976881c98c6c58b2ceeb0ff11c033f9926afd3.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(3,2)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(2,3)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(2,3)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=3.$\nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [1,5]$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-342581/field-1/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "7f2a9871-30c7-5f85-8f7c-27bc28d08d91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342581/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c90ef847-b4bf-5479-a810-e9c0d1177d9d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342581/field-1/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer-field/choices ["q-342581/field-1/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342581" :question/id #uuid "6cd4a653-e15a-56e6-a54e-5c8af74b1421" :question/math-academy-id "q-342581" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x}$ and the point $P(4,1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(1,4)$ is$\\,${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(4,1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{4}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(4,1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,4)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{8})} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-342581/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342581"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342583/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "0998d20c-9922-58be-aec2-a73bf65cba6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186295833 :answer-field/choices ["q-342583/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-2526/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "7224e289-6af6-598b-acd8-8752ff81d921" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2526/selection/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer/id #uuid "e44184de-708c-556e-b483-1c70619b588d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2526/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "c01b9e4d-9150-583f-9000-e05e952acf6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2526/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "057a795f-6f79-5b8c-8096-4ddee3f29aee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2526/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer/id #uuid "9ac85305-fe64-5710-a7c0-b59a5a1fac45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2526/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1a1d767c-4ca6-570e-8141-da96224c934b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-2526/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer-field/choices ["q-2526/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-2526/selection/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" "q-2526/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-2526/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" "q-2526/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-2526" :question/id #uuid "469ade2f-f4b3-58d6-a683-fc6ee5ddd73f" :question/math-academy-id "q-2526" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3x$ and the point $P(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-2526/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-2526"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-2294"] :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+3$ and the point $P(2,11)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(11).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(2,11).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}+3 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =12\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(2,11)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(11,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(11) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(11))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-110308/selection/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e" :answer/id #uuid "b7575923-a50b-5387-8c92-29289d6ae0e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110308/selection/2aef90071200a7bc65baa2d03f49803233ca7b2dc16865265136281ee914878b" :answer/id #uuid "32283e5a-58c1-5838-ae16-131a056cae36" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110308/selection/3089f9a34f7a805d8b2b2d5d51b10dc7aea027e8ec8fd8d06ad11cecf2ba9740" :answer/id #uuid "0443ee2b-27c2-5660-b22f-c7e876d28e6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110308/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer/id #uuid "270648f4-f099-56ad-a495-b9645d40f66a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110308/selection/b01ba7e917b2f2dc3ff90ccb2c435764c9ebef2dea263cc318e0c231eb7eeb1d" :answer/id #uuid "27b756d2-b835-573f-844a-24dd03d31203" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110308/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "efdfbf35-7dd9-5182-8244-71da7be1b9b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-110308/selection/b01ba7e917b2f2dc3ff90ccb2c435764c9ebef2dea263cc318e0c231eb7eeb1d" :answer-field/choices ["q-110308/selection/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e" "q-110308/selection/2aef90071200a7bc65baa2d03f49803233ca7b2dc16865265136281ee914878b" "q-110308/selection/3089f9a34f7a805d8b2b2d5d51b10dc7aea027e8ec8fd8d06ad11cecf2ba9740" "q-110308/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" "q-110308/selection/b01ba7e917b2f2dc3ff90ccb2c435764c9ebef2dea263cc318e0c231eb7eeb1d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-110308" :question/id #uuid "9f7f5d03-a853-5e3f-88b3-f143c3384e25" :question/math-academy-id "q-110308" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(3)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $5$ | $-2$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $-2$ | $3$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $-5$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(3)={g}^{′}(3){f}^{-1}(3)+\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(3)=-2,$ $g(3)=-3,$ ${g}^{′}(3)=1,$ and ${f}^{′}(-2)=-4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(3) & ={g}^{′}(3){f}^{-1}(3)+\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)+\\frac{-3}{{f}^{′}(-2)} \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{(-3)}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-110308/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-110308"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-110307/selection/edc901e84f0cdd5174afd49105ac3883566ea0e1e057217fb66083a56cc119ba" :answer/id #uuid "e98de654-05d8-50a7-b097-e31f2fd0c5a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110307/selection/e26ea8b4904e300d7c2b3c2f06aa53ea220babfdcaa7abe43a4c026367c79765" :answer/id #uuid "b908e1e5-7546-541e-8b71-08859b50e757" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110307/selection/2d8ae85212023567913a414489d5205c33db213bce0e85f85a24e6dff779b8a6" :answer/id #uuid "157fc464-b501-5e51-9aa0-735d660c5d85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{11}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110307/selection/75be7144d0f06979df25a4d833026b673a3f8e67075b82357bb7a90b42abaa01" :answer/id #uuid "f48bbaab-5ab6-5c21-baf4-cca447254bb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110307/selection/19d5466e84349f416e7f242f3eae0e8ce9f2558634cf0796beb62f8e60f5ea80" :answer/id #uuid "c92cfc0c-0b9d-5ea7-b010-72abd6d1d49a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110307/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "64bafd13-4937-5c24-b2ed-715d4d5a8781" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-110307/selection/e26ea8b4904e300d7c2b3c2f06aa53ea220babfdcaa7abe43a4c026367c79765" :answer-field/choices ["q-110307/selection/edc901e84f0cdd5174afd49105ac3883566ea0e1e057217fb66083a56cc119ba" "q-110307/selection/e26ea8b4904e300d7c2b3c2f06aa53ea220babfdcaa7abe43a4c026367c79765" "q-110307/selection/2d8ae85212023567913a414489d5205c33db213bce0e85f85a24e6dff779b8a6" "q-110307/selection/75be7144d0f06979df25a4d833026b673a3f8e67075b82357bb7a90b42abaa01" "q-110307/selection/19d5466e84349f416e7f242f3eae0e8ce9f2558634cf0796beb62f8e60f5ea80"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-110307" :question/id #uuid "728516cc-a318-5bc6-87a9-bed32f2e0ec8" :question/math-academy-id "q-110307" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)$  of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n| $-3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(4)={g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4)+\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(4)=-3,$ $g(4)=3,$ ${g}^{′}(4)=1,$ and ${f}^{′}(-3)=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(4) & ={g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4)+\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-3)+\\frac{3}{{f}^{′}(-3)} \\\\  & =-3+\\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-110307/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-110307"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342597/field-1/3a619fab2616dab0463f9c76cb72cd9f101e45e62111356a5b0b111e062a8dcf" :answer/id #uuid "4c011dca-d08a-55e4-a0ce-0ee30927aa38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342597/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "3c58aabf-fc98-57de-8141-7ea37cba24d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342597/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c9d1ac83-e100-52eb-aaef-8007d5f0ddcf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342597/field-1/3a619fab2616dab0463f9c76cb72cd9f101e45e62111356a5b0b111e062a8dcf" :answer-field/choices ["q-342597/field-1/3a619fab2616dab0463f9c76cb72cd9f101e45e62111356a5b0b111e062a8dcf" "q-342597/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342597" :question/id #uuid "41d98065-acca-5eb2-82b3-4c1ae5f6a004" :question/math-academy-id "q-342597" :question/problem "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $4$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(0)={g}^{′}(0){f}^{-1}(0)+\\frac{g(0)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(0)=-3,$ $g(0)=1,$ ${g}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{3},$ and ${f}^{′}(-3)=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & ={g}^{′}(0){f}^{-1}(0)+\\frac{g(0)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-3)+\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-3)} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-342597/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342597"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-110497/selection/e77b5bb73e42475d6f60b78fb5aaf572edb7ee92ac2b24278a86eeed6734b061" :answer/id #uuid "a61fce5a-e8b2-551e-be81-c08266127b85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110497/selection/b77f430e2a7875f4f1c1280b59d2b01911bbac2780d71cc0f48e5ec055c76b50" :answer/id #uuid "ff84c467-f387-5c94-9dd6-43c96bf71358" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110497/selection/d1b81b042eec2f31bc72eb579aaaeb48ffc21dce500509abe747d7486af70985" :answer/id #uuid "471cad84-e584-5ae6-9ac1-de074e965d43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110497/selection/92f4a11efc6c507a9ce8ab91917166320f553f732d68397990860325055044ae" :answer/id #uuid "480b4459-f881-5842-b5e9-cea3977473e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110497/selection/9332ac2648b3c5fbb5d6b2c3df5d41eaa4d565ab1193d3ae6d35f5952cbbd70c" :answer/id #uuid "26a44af3-d49f-5e88-a79a-779e5caf3e82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110497/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "37c6be68-8840-526d-bb62-7a0a60f78f74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-110497/selection/e77b5bb73e42475d6f60b78fb5aaf572edb7ee92ac2b24278a86eeed6734b061" :answer-field/choices ["q-110497/selection/e77b5bb73e42475d6f60b78fb5aaf572edb7ee92ac2b24278a86eeed6734b061" "q-110497/selection/b77f430e2a7875f4f1c1280b59d2b01911bbac2780d71cc0f48e5ec055c76b50" "q-110497/selection/d1b81b042eec2f31bc72eb579aaaeb48ffc21dce500509abe747d7486af70985" "q-110497/selection/92f4a11efc6c507a9ce8ab91917166320f553f732d68397990860325055044ae" "q-110497/selection/9332ac2648b3c5fbb5d6b2c3df5d41eaa4d565ab1193d3ae6d35f5952cbbd70c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-110497" :question/id #uuid "cd6d2ebf-c817-5eeb-8275-41ef01d7d0f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-110497" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={3}^{x}-1.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(8).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(8),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(8)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(8))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(8).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(8)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(8) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =8 \\\\ {3}^{x}-1 & =8 \\\\ {3}^{x} & =9 \\\\ {3}^{x} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(8)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={3}^{x}-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot {3}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(8))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-110497/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-110497"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-76115/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "4ebcd5f2-d502-5f14-b4c8-58bda3316f25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76115/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "22a78c11-6322-5d7a-94d8-28662b7d9bb9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76115/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "8139a2ba-0431-5b1f-bfb8-efbdacc2e356" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76115/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "f50fc084-f9c3-55ce-be8a-d3f4aaa6d598" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76115/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer/id #uuid "106944ca-a68d-5131-99a4-0e2271631df1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76115/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "dcee70ef-3e60-5eb7-8716-131a1e19306d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-76115/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-76115/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-76115/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-76115/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-76115/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-76115/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-76115" :question/id #uuid "7f01d7ed-1d8a-5bb3-a9a6-4686958c8b4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-76115" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ For $x>\\frac{1}{2},$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} & =1 \\\\ 1 & =\\sqrt{2x-1} \\\\ 1 & =2x-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(1)=1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(2x-1)^{3/2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{1}{(2\\cdot 1-1)^{3/2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-76115/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-76115"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-110345/selection/42168cb281f7e23cbe2f7237560107df465f00e00e21670946c2e9750154911f" :answer/id #uuid "b9773492-dd72-5047-9ee5-cb02d71d9e6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110345/selection/954c6419a6ce3207202fb6189cde6590b72373b92b62015c961ad9bb2ce0991e" :answer/id #uuid "b06a95dc-1400-50f5-8af4-7eda09d1a504" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110345/selection/5b7bb5121c5bd321353031595bf069bc6bffd3d8c4bc63b4cb89e72f4f0ed750" :answer/id #uuid "d0d77215-4d73-53d9-bb8d-66dbeec46e0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110345/selection/9332ac2648b3c5fbb5d6b2c3df5d41eaa4d565ab1193d3ae6d35f5952cbbd70c" :answer/id #uuid "345f13cb-9c41-55bd-b227-0e7bd5ad4c57" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110345/selection/5887ebcdbc1cf0d559424362ef725d715dd905a1f74eb78cdb2e14d4b13c3fb5" :answer/id #uuid "eacd902e-01d0-5056-8826-5245394852c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110345/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "32082d83-d392-5ea3-8624-0af973a39806" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-110345/selection/5b7bb5121c5bd321353031595bf069bc6bffd3d8c4bc63b4cb89e72f4f0ed750" :answer-field/choices ["q-110345/selection/42168cb281f7e23cbe2f7237560107df465f00e00e21670946c2e9750154911f" "q-110345/selection/954c6419a6ce3207202fb6189cde6590b72373b92b62015c961ad9bb2ce0991e" "q-110345/selection/5b7bb5121c5bd321353031595bf069bc6bffd3d8c4bc63b4cb89e72f4f0ed750" "q-110345/selection/9332ac2648b3c5fbb5d6b2c3df5d41eaa4d565ab1193d3ae6d35f5952cbbd70c" "q-110345/selection/5887ebcdbc1cf0d559424362ef725d715dd905a1f74eb78cdb2e14d4b13c3fb5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-110345" :question/id #uuid "ab71d05c-b918-52c2-bf47-2e847a369a63" :question/math-academy-id "q-110345" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={2}^{x}-7.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(9).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(9),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(9)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(9))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(9).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(9)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(9) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =9 \\\\ {2}^{x}-7 & =9 \\\\ {2}^{x} & =16 \\\\ {2}^{x} & ={2}^{4} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(9)=4.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(4),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={2}^{x}-7 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =16\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(9) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(9))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-110345/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-110345"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-14720"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/assets/8fb287885c948b7e563b5fa878dd204dacbedea311490c4573aa883bff992662.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y=f^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2, 0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$\nThe point $B$ has coordinates $(0,2)$ and lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ If $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2},$ what is $(f^{-1})'(0)?$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
